window.docContents[223].resolve({"/Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy/What-a-description-logic-cannot-express/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy--What-a-description-logic-cannot-express":{"contents":"A description logic captures the taxonomy but is structurally unable to capture\nthe calculus. The recurring gap is arithmetic, operations,\ndefinedness-conditions, laws, and proofs:\n\n* Dimension arithmetic (): OWL2 has no\n  datatype arithmetic; in Lean it is an ordinary function with a homomorphism\n  theorem.* The interaction algebra (torque  angle  energy): role chains\n  are binary, regular, and carry no arithmetic side-conditions; in Lean it is a\n  ternary relation with a dimensional-coherence proof obligation.* Scale-type operator gating (which of  are defined): DL has\n  no operations; in Lean the scale indexes which typeclasses apply, and\n  monotonicity of availability is a proved meta-theorem.* Extensivity ():\n  no quantified arithmetic in DL; in Lean it is a -theorem.* Units (a unit is a chosen value of a kind; conversion is a ratio): no\n  arithmetic in DL; in Lean it is a definition with a round-trip theorem.* Meta-theorems (specialization is a preorder;  is a homomorphism): a\n  DL reasoner checks consistency, not laws — proving the laws is the point.\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint\u0009Why a calculus, not a taxonomy","header":"1.2. What a description logic cannot express","id":"/Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy/What-a-description-logic-cannot-express/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Why-a-calculus___-not-a-taxonomy--What-a-description-logic-cannot-express"}});